Now the plan document. Let me define the full strategy:

**Case**: Power-tool battery. Task: capacity ≥ 2 Ah; 5C retention ≥ 95%; 4C fast charge no plating; T_max ≤ 60°C; power density ≥ 4000 W/kg.

**System**: Chen2020 (NMC811/graphite, 5 Ah nominal, wound geometry 0.065×1.58 m). Default because task text specifies no electrode system (anchor table rule: no explicit system → Chen2020 with record).

**start_stage**: 3 (no new-material objective in task text — performance/architecture task). Materials use baseline parameters. Molecular funnel skipped; funnel log will state the base determination basis.

**Freedoms** (widest interpretation, task text declares none):
1. Electrode system: adjustable (base parameter-set switch allowed if needed — e.g., escalation path).
2. Electrolyte formulation: adjustable (σ/t⁺/D transport overrides with literature values).
3. Electrode modification: adjustable (SEI/cracking parameter overrides; likely unused here).
4. Cell architecture: adjustable (thickness/porosity/N-P/separator/CC/particle size/geometry).
5. Thermal management: adjustable (cooling h).

**Objective decomposition / trade-offs**:
- Power density 4000 W/kg: P/m = V_OC²/(4·DCR·m). Levers: thin electrodes (mass↓, DCR↓ — strongest lever, P/m ∝ ~1/L²), thin CC (mass↓), small particles (R_ct↓), high-κ electrolyte (R_ion↓), separator thin+porous.
- 5C retention ≥ 95%: rate capability — thin electrodes, small particles, high-κ electrolyte, higher porosity (ionic transport), moderate N/P.
- 4C no plating: anode margin — N/P ≥ 1.1, small anode particles (kinetic surface ↑), high-κ/high-D/high-t⁺ electrolyte, moderate anode thickness. Tension: thin anode raises plating risk; cathode thinning raises N/P at given anode.
- T_max ≤ 60°C (333.15 K): heat vs cooling. 4C charge at 45°C ambient is the binding constraint (only 15 K headroom). Reduce DCR (thin, high-κ) and raise h if needed (active air cooling h~20-30; contract default 10).
- Capacity ≥ 2 Ah: binding lower bound — limits electrode thinning (areal capacity), but 5 Ah baseline gives room; target designs keep 1C capacity ≥ 2 Ah (measured from full SOC).

**Candidate strategy**:
- Round 1 (baseline): Chen2020 as-is characterization: 1C discharge (as-is + charged-state), 5C discharge, 4C charge 45°C + plating, calc-energy → gap analysis vs criteria.
- Round 2+: architecture variants (2-4 per round):
  - V_thin: thinner electrodes (pos 75.6→50 μm, neg 85.2→60 μm) + smaller particles (pos 5.22→3.5 μm, neg 5.86→4 μm)
  - V_el: electrolyte transport boost (κ ×1.3, D ×1.5, t⁺ 0.2594→0.35 — literature: concentrated/advanced formulations; recorded as literature-based parameter override, source noted)
  - V_sep: separator 12→8 μm? (thinnest practical ~7 μm), porosity 0.47→0.5? (hmm sep porosity higher → less mass, less ionic resistance)
  - V_cc: Al 16→10 μm, Cu 12→8 μm (power-tool cells use thin CC)
  - V_cool: h 10→20-30
- Composite candidate rounds: combine passing levers; per-round evaluate with comparison table.
- Ceiling assessment in round 2-3: best-possible combo (all levers) vs objective → decide if architecture space suffices; if not → escalate to Stage 2 (electrolyte formulation candidates / system switch — e.g., OKane2022? no — power tools: keep NMC; maybe LFP? LFP has lower V_OC → lower power density; not helpful. Stay NMC811/graphite.)

**Budget**: ~8-12 rounds; per round 4-6 sims (SPMe for screen, DFN for high-rate + final).

**Risk & fallback**:
- Power density may be the hardest: if architecture+electrolyte space tops out < 4000 W/kg → check boundary assumptions (three-strike questioning): mass definition (calc-energy excludes electrolyte/casing — real power density would be LOWER with them — so contract-caliber 4000 is the honest goal), DCR definition (DC pulse vs 10%-discharge slope), system switch (OKane2022 SiOx for higher capacity? no — power density needs low mass... SiOx doesn't help power). Negative-result closure allowed with honest "if relaxed to X it becomes reachable" note.
- T_max: raise h as needed (freedom), thin electrodes (less heat), and note that 45°C ambient + 4C is aggressive.
- Plating: if plating persists → N/P ↑ (anode thicker/cathode thinner), anode particles ↓, t⁺/κ/D ↑, porosity ↑.
- 5C retention: if < 95% → thinner electrodes/particles/κ ↑.
- Capacity < 2 Ah → don't over-thin.

**References** (domain basis, real, no fabrication):
- Thin electrodes raise rate capability and lower DC resistance → domain experience (rate capability ∝ 1/L² for diffusion-limited electrodes; widely documented in electrode-engineering literature — no precise source).
- Small particle size reduces charge-transfer resistance (higher active surface area) → domain experience.
- High-conductivity electrolyte mitigates salt depletion and lithium plating in fast charge → general battery literature (e.g., lithium plating onset tied to electrolyte transport — domain experience, no precise source).
- Power-tool cells: high-rate NMC/graphite 18650/21700, 5-10C rated, thin coated electrodes → industry practice (cell datasheets, e.g., Sony VTC series; domain experience).
- N/P ratio ≥ 1.05-1.15 avoids plating at fast charge → domain experience (cell-design practice).
- Cooling: h=10 W/m²/K natural convection; forced air 15-30 → heat-transfer engineering practice (domain experience).

