# Design Plan — Power-Tool Battery (Case t3_r1)

## 1. Objective decomposition
Task (verbatim thresholds):
- nominal capacity ≥ 2 Ah (stage2, min 2.0)
- 5C discharge capacity retention ≥ 95% (stage2, min 0.95; = 5C cap ÷ same-params 1C cap)
- 4C fast charge without lithium plating (stage3, plated=false; anode surface potential ≥ 0 V throughout 4C charge at 45 °C)
- maximum temperature ≤ 60 °C (stage3, T_max_K max 333.15; judged on the hotter of the 5C-discharge (25 °C amb.) and 4C-charge (45 °C amb.) lumped-thermal runs)
- power density ≥ 4000 W/kg (stage2; calc-energy contract: V_OC²/(4·DCR)/mass)

Multi-objective trade-off expectations:
- Power density vs capacity/energy: power density P/m ≈ V²/(4·DCR·m) rises roughly with 1/L² when electrodes thin (both areal resistance and areal mass fall) — but thinner electrodes cut capacity (must stay ≥ 2 Ah). Thick → energy cell; thin → power cell; this case is a power-tool cell, so design pushes toward the thin side of the ≥2 Ah constraint.
- 5C retention vs capacity: same tension (thin = high retention).
- 4C plating vs 5C retention: plating risk falls with higher N/P (thicker anode relative to cathode) and smaller anode particles; both also help rate capability — aligned, not conflicting. Thinner cathode raises N/P → helps plating, hurts capacity (binding constraint again).
- T_max vs DCR: all heat-generation reductions (thin, high-κ electrolyte, small particles) reduce temperature rise; cooling h is the direct lever (thermal-management freedom).

## 2. System & boundary
- Base parameter set: Chen2020 (NMC811/graphite, nominal 5 Ah, wound geometry 0.065 m × 1.58 m) — task text names no electrode system → anchor-table default with record (dump verified: no SiOx → not OKane2022; SEI kinetics present; complete thermal/geometry present).
- start_stage = 3 (no new-material objective in task text; materials use system baseline).
- Degrees of freedom (task text declares none → widest interpretation, recorded in entry 0 meta.freedoms): electrode system / electrolyte formulation / electrode modification / cell architecture / thermal management all adjustable.
- Measurement notes: discharge protocols use fully-charged initial state (known Chen2020 discharged-initial-state artifact); 4C charge starts from the set's default state via the runner's discharge-first experiment.

## 3. Candidate strategy
- Round 1: baseline characterization (Chen2020 defaults; + artifact-documentation run from the set's own initial state).
- Round 2: single-lever architecture variants (thin electrodes / small particles / fast electrolyte / thin CC+separator / cooling h) — attribute each metric's driver.
- Rounds 3+: composite candidates combining passing levers; final refinement on the binding metric.
- Ceiling assessment: after rounds 1–2, compare best-possible architecture+formulation vs objective; if the gap is transport/material-bound → escalate to Stage 2 (electrolyte formulation candidates / system switch), not more tuning.

## 4. Budget allocation
~10 rounds; per candidate: 1C DFN + 5C DFN + 4C DFN(lumped+plating) + calc-energy + derived metrics + batch log-evaluate. SPMe only for quick screens.

## 5. Risk and fallback plan
- Power density 4000 W/kg is aggressive for a 5-Ah-class cell → if architecture+electrolyte space tops out below threshold: three-strike questioning (boundary/mass-definition/system), then honest negative result with "reachable if relaxed to X" note.
- Plating at 4C → fallback Stage 3: N/P ↑, anode particle ↓, t⁺/κ/D ↑, porosity ↑.
- 5C retention < 95% → fallback Stage 3: thinner electrodes, smaller particles, electrolyte κ ↑.
- T_max > 333.15 K → fallback Stage 3: h ↑ (cooling design), DCR ↓.
- Capacity < 2 Ah → do not thin further; check N/P and cutoffs.

## 6. Domain basis (no fabricated references)
- Thin electrodes → rate capability and power density ↑ (electrode engineering practice — domain experience, no precise source)
- Small particles → charge-transfer resistance ↓ (higher active area — domain experience)
- High-conductivity/high-t⁺ electrolyte → salt-depletion and plating suppression in fast charge (battery-transport literature — domain experience)
- Power-tool cells: high-rate NMC/graphite cylindrical/pouch, 5–10 C rated, thin electrodes (industry practice, e.g., Sony VTC-class cells — domain experience)
- N/P ≈ 1.05–1.15 typical fast-charge margin (cell-design practice — domain experience)
- Natural convection h ≈ 10 W/m²/K; forced air ≈ 15–30 (heat-transfer engineering practice — domain experience)

